MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 2018873941}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'The Three Estates were a way of describing the social structure of medieval
    European society. Society was broadly divided into three major groups: the First
    Estate, the Second Estate, and the Third Estate. Each group had different roles,
    responsibilities, privileges, and ways of life.

    The First Estate consisted
    mainly of the clergy, including bishops, priests, monks, and other members of
    the Church. Religion played an important role in medieval life. The Church conducted
    religious ceremonies, maintained monasteries and schools, and influenced many
    aspects of society. Members of the clergy were also involved in education, charity,
    and administration.

    The Second Estate consisted of the nobility, including
    kings, lords, knights, and other landowning nobles. Land was an important source
    of wealth and power. Nobles governed territories, managed estates, collected
    revenues, and often provided military service. Knights played an important role
    in warfare and protecting territories.

    The Third Estate included the common
    people, who formed the largest and most diverse group. This group included peasants,
    farmers, artisans, merchants, laborers, and other workers. Farmers produced food,
    artisans created goods, and merchants participated in trade. Their work supported
    much of the economic life of medieval communities.

    The Three Estates can
    be visualized as a social pyramid, showing the different levels and roles within
    society. However, the exact structure varied across regions and periods, so the
    pyramid is a simplified educational representation rather than a universal system.

    Learning
    about the Three Estates helps us understand how religion, land, government, warfare,
    agriculture, crafts, and trade were connected in medieval Europe. It also shows
    how different groups contributed to society while experiencing very different
    social positions and responsibilities.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
